1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,176
The last episode covered the four steps of CALL.

2
00:00:09,176 --> 00:00:14,133
In this one, we follow a real program all the way through.

3
00:00:14,133 --> 00:00:15,361
First, a quick quiz.

4
00:00:15,361 --> 00:00:21,933
For the two instructions below, after which step is the machine code completely settled?

5
00:00:21,933 --> 00:00:27,724
In step one, the compiler translates the C code into assembly, giving hello.s.

6
00:00:27,724 --> 00:00:35,233
Notice that la, call, li, and ret are all pseudo-instructions, left for the assembler to expand.

7
00:00:35,233 --> 00:00:38,923
Everything that begins with a dot is an assembler directive.

8
00:00:38,923 --> 00:00:45,266
It produces no machine instruction, and only tells the assembler how to organize the object file.

9
00:00:45,266 --> 00:00:52,466
The .text directive says that code comes next, and .align 2 aligns to four bytes, which is two squared.

10
00:00:52,466 --> 00:01:00,333
The .global directive, also written .globl, declares main as a global symbol, so other files can refer to it.

11
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:06,233
Next we switch to the read-only data section, .rodata, again aligned to four bytes.

12
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:13,433
The .string directive stores strings that end in a zero, and the labels str1 and str2 mark where they start.

13
00:01:13,433 --> 00:01:16,438
Two other common directives aren't used here.

14
00:01:16,438 --> 00:01:24,566
The .data directive enters the data segment, and .word stores thirty-two bit words one after another.

15
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:28,609
In step two, the assembler produces the object file hello.o.

16
00:01:28,609 --> 00:01:34,300
It is binary, so looked at directly it is just a string of hexadecimal numbers.

17
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:35,799
Let's disassemble main.

18
00:01:35,799 --> 00:01:41,342
The left column holds addresses within the module, the middle holds the machine code,

19
00:01:41,342 --> 00:01:44,212
and the right column holds the instructions.

20
00:01:44,212 --> 00:01:53,000
All the pseudo-instructions have been expanded, though this listing is simplified, and call becomes only one jalr.

21
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,484
But the immediates of these five instructions are all zero.

22
00:01:56,484 --> 00:02:02,213
They are just placeholders, because the assembler doesn't yet know where the data and printf are.

23
00:02:02,213 --> 00:02:08,000
The other instructions involve no address, so once assembled, their machine code is settled.

24
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:13,951
The symbol table records this file's labels and their addresses within their own segments,

25
00:02:13,951 --> 00:02:16,199
and the debugger gdb needs it too.

26
00:02:16,199 --> 00:02:23,633
The label main is at offset zero in the code segment, with type global, which is exactly what .global does.

27
00:02:23,633 --> 00:02:25,625
The two strings are local data.

28
00:02:25,625 --> 00:02:33,366
The first one, including its terminating zero, takes twelve bytes, so the second one starts at 0xc.

29
00:02:33,366 --> 00:02:39,343
The relocation table is a to-do list for the linker, with an entry for every placeholder.

30
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:46,595
The first two entries are the lui at 0x8, which is waiting for the upper twenty bits of the address of str1.

31
00:02:46,595 --> 00:02:50,155
The addi at 0xc is waiting for the lower twelve bits.

32
00:02:50,155 --> 00:02:59,033
The two entries for str2 work the same way, and the last one, at 0x18, is a jalr waiting for the address of printf.

33
00:02:59,033 --> 00:03:04,406
Why can the assembler settle some addresses, while others must wait for the linker?

34
00:03:04,406 --> 00:03:06,866
Because addresses come in three kinds.

35
00:03:06,866 --> 00:03:12,562
First, PC-relative jumps within the file, such as beq, or a jal to a label in this file,

36
00:03:12,562 --> 00:03:17,400
where the distance stays the same wherever the code as a whole moves.

37
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:24,397
Second, the addresses of static data, such as the str1 that la loads, or a global variable that lw reads,

38
00:03:24,397 --> 00:03:27,862
can't be known until all the files are put together.

39
00:03:27,862 --> 00:03:32,060
Third, external functions, such as printf, are in another file,

40
00:03:32,060 --> 00:03:35,858
so the assembler doesn't even know how far away they are.

41
00:03:35,858 --> 00:03:42,256
The last two kinds go into the relocation table, which is why a jal sometimes has to be changed,

42
00:03:42,256 --> 00:03:48,100
while a B-type branch only jumps within the module and never needs to.

43
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:52,207
In step three, the linker puts hello.o, the startup routine,

44
00:03:52,207 --> 00:03:57,166
and the printf from the library together into the executable a.out.

45
00:03:57,166 --> 00:03:58,210
Where is printf?

46
00:03:58,210 --> 00:04:03,496
The linker looks through your own .o files first, and then through the libraries,

47
00:04:03,496 --> 00:04:07,282
and a .a library file is really just a bundle of .o files.

48
00:04:07,282 --> 00:04:14,395
Linking also makes separate compilation possible, so if you change hello.c, only that file needs recompiling,

49
00:04:14,395 --> 00:04:18,033
and the huge C library doesn't have to be rebuilt.

50
00:04:18,033 --> 00:04:27,630
First the code segments are joined end to end from 0x10000, with the startup routine first, then main, and then printf.

51
00:04:27,630 --> 00:04:35,100
The data segment follows the code, with str1 at 0x20A10, and str2 twelve bytes after it.

52
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:40,933
With the final addresses of all the symbols settled, the linker updates the symbol table to match.

53
00:04:40,933 --> 00:04:46,267
Then, entry by entry, it fills in the holes according to the relocation table.

54
00:04:46,267 --> 00:04:49,686
First it puts in each instruction's final address.

55
00:04:49,686 --> 00:04:55,841
Starting with str1, which is 0x20A10, the lower twelve bits, 0xA10, have a top bit of one,

56
00:04:55,841 --> 00:05:01,791
so addi would treat them as the negative number minus one thousand five hundred twenty.

57
00:05:01,791 --> 00:05:09,733
So the upper twenty bits must be one higher, written 0x21, which is exactly how li was split in episode twelve.

58
00:05:09,733 --> 00:05:16,789
Now we put 0x21 into the immediate field of the lui, so the placeholder zeros turn into real address bits.

59
00:05:16,789 --> 00:05:24,200
And with minus one thousand five hundred twenty filled into the addi, these two instructions are complete.

60
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:32,995
For str2 it is the same, with the lui also filled with 0x21, and the addi with minus one thousand five hundred eight.

61
00:05:32,995 --> 00:05:38,106
Last is printf, which is at 0x10450, and 0x288 bytes from this call.

62
00:05:38,106 --> 00:05:47,333
A jal can jump about one mebibyte either way, which is plenty, so the linker rewrites this directly into a single jal.

63
00:05:47,333 --> 00:05:50,892
None of the other instructions changed by a single bit.

64
00:05:50,892 --> 00:05:58,400
With the file header and debugging information added, a.out is complete, and every bit is settled.

65
00:05:58,400 --> 00:05:59,617
Back to the quiz.

66
00:05:59,617 --> 00:06:03,985
The add involves no address, so it is settled after assembly.

67
00:06:03,985 --> 00:06:13,233
But the jal jumps to fprintf, an external function in the stdio library, so it isn't settled until after linking.

68
00:06:13,233 --> 00:06:16,017
What we just did is called static linking.

69
00:06:16,017 --> 00:06:22,779
The library code is copied right into a.out, so the program is self-sufficient, but the file is large.

70
00:06:22,779 --> 00:06:30,033
And once the library fixes a bug or a security hole, every program has to be relinked and redistributed.

71
00:06:30,033 --> 00:06:35,169
Dynamic linking instead stores the library as a separate file, such as libc.so,

72
00:06:35,169 --> 00:06:38,290
and links it in only when the program is loaded.

73
00:06:38,290 --> 00:06:44,597
The program files are smaller, several programs can share the same copy of the library in memory,

74
00:06:44,597 --> 00:06:47,718
and replacing libc.so upgrades everyone at once.

75
00:06:47,718 --> 00:06:52,660
The cost is link overhead at run time, and an a.out alone can no longer run,

76
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:57,066
but overall the benefits still outweigh the costs.

77
00:06:57,066 --> 00:07:02,391
As a bonus, which isn't on the exam, a program doesn't have to be translated at all,

78
00:07:02,391 --> 00:07:07,082
but can be handed to an interpreter, another program, to execute directly.

79
00:07:07,082 --> 00:07:13,041
Python relies entirely on interpretation, which makes it the easiest to write and the slowest.

80
00:07:13,041 --> 00:07:18,400
Java is first compiled to bytecode and then interpreted, so it runs everywhere.

81
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:21,705
C is compiled to machine code, which is much faster.

82
00:07:21,705 --> 00:07:29,433
Machine code is the hardest to write by hand, but the best to interpret, since the hardware can execute it directly.

83
00:07:29,433 --> 00:07:32,862
Machine code can also be interpreted by software.

84
00:07:32,862 --> 00:07:39,090
Venus simulates RISC-V one instruction at a time, which makes single-step debugging easy.

85
00:07:39,090 --> 00:07:46,018
Apple has changed its ISA several times, each time using software to run programs from the old one,

86
00:07:46,018 --> 00:07:48,266
and the latest was Rosetta.

87
00:07:48,266 --> 00:07:51,955
Interpreters are easy to write, easy to debug, and portable,

88
00:07:51,955 --> 00:07:59,833
while translated programs are usually more than ten times faster, and don't have to reveal their source code.

89
00:07:59,833 --> 00:08:06,483
Putting it together, the program is compiled into assembly, assembled into machine code with placeholders,

90
00:08:06,483 --> 00:08:10,247
linked to fill in the addresses, and finally loaded and run.

91
00:08:10,247 --> 00:08:16,332
The loader reads a.out into memory, and through the startup routine calls main, and Hello, world!

92
00:08:16,332 --> 00:08:18,066
appears on the screen.

93
00:08:18,066 --> 00:08:24,036
This is also a miniature of the whole series, from registers, memory, branches, and functions,

94
00:08:24,036 --> 00:08:26,131
to instruction encoding and CALL.

95
00:08:26,131 --> 00:08:33,866
From a single line of printf to a string of settled bits, this is the road a program travels before it runs.

